    "note": "A **holiday** in American English is one day of celebration: *a public holiday*; a longer time off is a **vacation**: *a two-week vacation*. British English uses **holiday** for both. A **break** is a short time of rest: *a weekend break*, *a coffee break*. **Leave** is time off allowed by an employer: *sick leave*, *on leave*."
  },
  "usage_note": "In British English you go **on holiday**, with no **a** and no **the**; American English says **on vacation**. *Happy holidays* is a greeting at the end of the year that does not name a particular celebration.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "We went to holiday in the mountains.",
      "correct": "We went on holiday in the mountains.",
      "note": "The phrase is **go on holiday** (British) or **go on vacation** (American)."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I am in holidays this week.",
      "correct": "I am on holiday this week.",
      "note": "Use **on**, and in this phrase **holiday** is usually singular."
    }
